    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the requirement.\n\n**How should incident response work?** should reduce blast radius first, preserve evidence, rotate/revoke exposed credentials, recover safely, and add a regression control.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method:\nDefine the source of truth, boundary, success path, failure path, and ownership. Choose the smallest design that satisfies the requirement before introducing additional infrastructure.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\nThe manual example exposes the core algorithm, state transition, lookup, or policy so it is easy to explain in a senior interview.\n\n```ts\ninterface Policy {\n  subject: string;\n  allowed: boolean;\n}\n\nfunction isAllowed(\n  policies: readonly Policy[],\n  subject: string,\n): boolean {\n  for (let i = 0; i < policies.length; i += 1) {\n    if (policies[i].subject === subject) {\n      return policies[i].allowed;\n    }\n  }\n\n  return false;\n}\n\nconst allowed = isAllowed(\n  [{ subject: \"current-user\", allowed: true }],\n  \"current-user\",\n);\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / practical TypeScript:\nUse native collections or platform APIs when they improve readability without hiding an important architectural trade-off.\n\n```ts\nconst allowedSubjects = new Set([\n  \"current-user\",\n]);\n\nconst allowed =\n  allowedSubjects.has(\"current-user\");\n```\n\nStep 5 — Production example:\nUse **How should incident response work?** with an explicit owner, failure path, measurement strategy, and migration plan.\n\nStep 6 — Complexity and scale:\nExplain the dominant cost and how it changes with growth, retries, users, data, events, or deployments. Then describe what you would cache, batch, partition, isolate, or measure.\n\nStep 7 — Edge cases:\nHandle empty state, partial failure, stale data, retries, cancellation, unauthorized access, slow network, rollout failure, and recovery where relevant.\n\nStep 8 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain the boundary, source of truth, failure/recovery path, observability signal, and key trade-off.",
